This is the key evidence. The CRI-O issue is no longer the active problem.

Root cause

Your SR-IOV Operator is stuck in the drain phase.

The most important lines are:

SYNC STATUS          InProgress
DESIRED SYNC STATE   Reboot_Required
CURRENT SYNC STATE   Draining

and

Warning  DrainController
node drain operation was not completed

This explains everything you've been seeing:

The node gets cordoned again after you uncordon it.

The MachineConfigPool remains paused.

The Machine Config Operator reports:

Failed to resync ... MachineConfigPool master was paused

The annotation:

sriovnetwork.openshift.io/state: Paused

is present because the SR-IOV operator is controlling the MCP.


Why is the drain not completing?

On a Single Node OpenShift (SNO) cluster, draining the only node is special. If there are pods that cannot be evicted (for example, because of PodDisruptionBudgets, static control plane pods, or other constraints), the SR-IOV operator can remain stuck in the Draining state.

The next thing to check

Please run:

oc logs -n openshift-sriov-network-operator deploy/sriov-network-operator --tail=200

and

oc get pods -A -o wide | grep Terminating

Also check whether there are any pending drain operations:

oc adm drain sno1.lab1.ocp.lan --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data --dry-run=client

This won't drain the node; it will help show what would block eviction.

One thing I also noticed

Your SriovOperatorConfig shows:

disableDrain: false

That means the SR-IOV Operator is configured to automatically drain nodes before applying changes.

On SNO systems, if your operational procedure doesn't require automatic draining, some environments set:

spec:
  disableDrain: true

This avoids the operator attempting to drain the only node. Whether that's appropriate depends on your environment and Red Hat's guidance for your SR-IOV deployment, so I wouldn't recommend changing it until we confirm that's the intended configuration.

